<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Hamdanid sultanate of Yemen</h1><p class="st-meta">1099 CE – 1174 CE (approx.) · capital Sanaa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A dynasty of the Hamdan tribe that ruled Sanaa in the 12th century after the breakup of the Sulayhid state.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sulayhid-dynasty">Sulayhid dynasty</a> lost control of the highlands, leaders of the Hamdan tribe took Sanaa. In 1099 Hatim ibn al-Ghashim established the dynasty that ruled the city, with brief interruptions, for most of the 12th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Three branches of the family succeeded one another in Sanaa. They were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Isma'ilis</a> loyal to the Hafizi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> and spent much effort fighting Zaydi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> to the north and local rivals. Their state was essentially a tribal confederation around Sanaa and its forts. Little is known of its administration beyond chronicles of war.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1174 the Ayyubid prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turan-shah-ibn-ayyub">Turan-Shah</a> invaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-hatim">Ali ibn Hatim</a> fled to the mountains, and by the 1180s Sanaa was under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ayyubid-yemen">Ayyubid Yemen</a>. The dynasty's fall marked the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Isma'ili</a> political rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1099 CE – 1174 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Arabia and the Gulf</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.57%;width:1.49%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1099 CE – 1174 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sanaa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hatim ibn al-Ghashim, Hatim ibn Ahmad, Ali ibn Hatim</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Isma'ili Shi'a Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hamdanids of Yemen, Banu Hatim, Hatimids</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1136&amp;focus=hamdanid-sultanate-of-yemen" aria-label="Hamdanid sultanate of Yemen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5887 1607 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hamdanid sultanate of Yemen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6207 1863L6207 1863M6207 1863L5811 1337"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6207 1863h0M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6207 1863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6207 1863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sanaa. <a href="/atlas?year=1136&amp;focus=hamdanid-sultanate-of-yemen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
